    "patron": "Sailors, merchants, bakers, travellers, and children; Greece, Russia, the Kingdom of Naples, Sicily, Lorraine, Liège, Campen, Corfu, Freiburg, and Moscow (Catholic Encyclopedia, 1911)",
    "relic_description": "<p>The relics of Saint Nicholas have been in Bari, Italy, since 9 May 1087, when sailors from Bari took them from his tomb at Myra. Pope Urban II later placed them beneath the main altar of the new basilica built over them. Between 1953 and 1957 the relics were re-examined and laid in a new tomb in the crypt. Each year on 9 May the clergy draw from the tomb a fragrant liquid that the faithful call the manna of Saint Nicholas. In 1100 a Venetian fleet took from Myra the remains that the men of Bari had left; Butler says these included only fragments, the remains of two other bishops, and some oil.</p>\n<p>Sources: <a href=\"https://sourcebooks.web.fordham.edu/basis/nicholas-bari.asp\">Greek Anonymous account of the Translation</a>; <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/12/history-of-veneration-of-saint-nicholas.html\">“History of the Veneration of Saint Nicholas of Myra”</a>; Alban Butler, <em>Lives of the Saints</em>, 6 Dec.</p>",
    "relic_location": "Basilica of St Nicholas, Bari, Italy (crypt)"
  },
  "related": {
    "miracles": [
      {
        "miracles": "<p>This list gathers the miracles attributed to Saint Nicholas of Myra, with the source of each account and its status. Nicholas lived in the fourth century, long before the Church held formal inquiries into miracles, so none of these accounts was examined by a canonization process. Some are honored in the liturgy of the Catholic and Orthodox Churches, some are recorded by the shrine at Bari, and some are popular tradition. Each entry names the oldest source that the research found for it. The list makes no claim of scientific proof, and it does not dismiss any account.</p>\n<h2 id=\"during-his-life\">During Nicholas’s life</h2>\n<h3 id=\"m01\">Three innocent men saved at Byrra</h3>\n<p>The governor Eustathius condemned three innocent men of Myra to death. Nicholas ran to the place of execution at Byrra, took the sword from the executioner as it was raised, and set the men free. He then went to the governor, refused his greeting, named bribery as the real cause, and later forgave him at the request of three imperial officers. This is the oldest known story of Nicholas, from the <em>Praxis de stratelatis</em>, which scholars date between the late fourth and the late sixth century. Status: the one story of Nicholas named in the Roman Martyrology; honored in the liturgy of both Churches.</p>\n<p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/12/stratelatis-earliest-known-account-of.html\"><em>Praxis de stratelatis</em>, trans. Charles W. Jones</a>; <a href=\"https://www.stnicholascenter.org/who-is-st-nicholas/real-person/nicholas-of-patara\">Gerardo Cioffari, “Nicholas of Patara, Bishop of Myra”</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m02\">Three generals saved through dreams, Constantinople</h3>\n<p>The three officers who had been in Myra at the time, Nepotianus, Ursus, and Eupoleonis, were later framed in Constantinople and condemned. They prayed to the God of Saint Nicholas. That night Nicholas appeared in a dream to the emperor Constantine and to the prefect Ablabius, named himself a sinner and the bishop of Myra, and the emperor set the officers free. The story is in the <em>Praxis de stratelatis</em>, in a sermon attributed to Proclus of Constantinople, and in a quotation by Eustratios of Constantinople after 583. Status: liturgical and popular tradition; the frame (Nepotianus and Ablabius) is historical according to Cioffari.</p>\n<p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/12/stratelatis-earliest-known-account-of.html\"><em>Praxis de stratelatis</em>, trans. Jones</a>; <a href=\"https://www.stnicholascenter.org/who-is-st-nicholas/stories-legends/classic-sources/saint-proclus\">Proclus (attributed), <em>Encomium on Saint Nicholas</em></a>; <a href=\"https://www.stnicholascenter.org/who-is-st-nicholas/stories-legends/classic-sources/literary-development\">Roger Pearse, “Literary Development of the Life of St Nicholas”</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m03\">Gold for three daughters</h3>\n<p>A man in Nicholas’s city had three daughters and no dowry for them. Nicholas threw a bag of gold through his window at night, then a second, and on the third night the father stayed awake and caught him. Nicholas made him swear to tell no one. The first full telling is in the Life by Michael the Archimandrite (early ninth century), but Cioffari reports three very old versions: the oldest has two girls and a boy, and another has four girls. Status: popular and liturgical tradition; the source of Nicholas’s patronage of gift-giving.</p>\n<p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/12/the-life-of-saint-nicholas-wonderworker.html\">Michael the Archimandrite, <em>Life</em>, secs. 10–18</a>; <a href=\"https://www.stnicholascenter.org/who-is-st-nicholas/real-person/nicholas-of-patara\">Cioffari, “Nicholas of Patara”</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m04\">Grain ships at Andriake</h3>\n<p>During a famine at Myra, Alexandrian grain ships bound for Constantinople lay at Andriake. Nicholas asked each to unload a hundred measures and promised that they would suffer no loss. Michael says that the cargo was found unchanged at Constantinople, and that the grain fed Myra for two years. Andrew of Crete (early eighth century) adds that Nicholas stood among the sailors and left three gold coins on the ship. Status: popular tradition; Cioffari calls the story generic and thinks it was in circulation by the sixth century.</p>\n<p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/12/the-life-of-saint-nicholas-wonderworker.html\">Michael the Archimandrite, <em>Life</em>, secs. 37–39</a>; <a href=\"https://www.stnicholascenter.org/who-is-st-nicholas/stories-legends/classic-sources/andrew-crete\">Andrew of Crete, <em>Encomium</em></a>; <a href=\"https://www.stnicholascenter.org/who-is-st-nicholas/real-person/nicholas-of-patara\">Cioffari</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m05\">Sailors in a storm who called his name</h3>\n<p>Sailors caught in a storm called on Nicholas. He appeared among them and said, “Behold, you have called me, and I am here to help you,” and they were saved. Later they found him in the church at Myra, dressed like one of the many clergy, and recognized him; he told them that their greed and lust invited God’s correction. Status: popular tradition and the origin of his patronage of sailors. Cioffari thinks the story fits Nicholas of Sion better, but it stands in Michael’s Life of the bishop of Myra.</p>\n<p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/12/the-life-of-saint-nicholas-wonderworker.html\">Michael the Archimandrite, <em>Life</em>, secs. 34–36</a>; <a href=\"https://www.stnicholascenter.org/who-is-st-nicholas/real-person/nicholas-of-patara\">Cioffari</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m06\">The temple of Artemis at Myra</h3>\n<p>Michael writes that Nicholas tore down the temple of Artemis at Myra, the tallest of the temples there, and that the demons who lived in it fled. Ancient inscriptions from Myra support the size and fame of the temple, according to Cioffari. Status: popular tradition.</p>\n<p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/12/the-life-of-saint-nicholas-wonderworker.html\">Michael the Archimandrite, <em>Life</em>, secs. 28–29</a>; <a href=\"https://www.stnicholascenter.org/who-is-st-nicholas/real-person/nicholas-of-patara\">Cioffari</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"after-his-death\">After Nicholas’s death</h2>\n<h3 id=\"m07\">Fragrant oil from the tomb at Myra</h3>\n<p>Michael writes that after Nicholas was buried in his church, his body gave off a sweet-smelling perfume. The <em>Golden Legend</em> (1275) says that a fountain of oil sprang from his head to his feet and that oil still flowed from his body. Status: a phenomenon reported for centuries at Myra and later at Bari.</p>\n<p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/12/the-life-of-saint-nicholas-wonderworker.html\">Michael the Archimandrite, <em>Life</em>, sec. 41</a>; <a href=\"https://www.stnicholascenter.org/who-is-st-nicholas/stories-legends/classic-sources/the-golden-legend\">Jacobus de Voragine, <em>The Golden Legend</em></a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m08\">The demon’s flask of oil</h3>\n<p>Michael’s Life tells, among the wonders after Nicholas’s death, of pilgrims bound for Myra and a flask of oil from a demon that was thrown into the sea. The research notes record only the outline of this story. Status: popular tradition.</p>\n<p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/12/the-life-of-saint-nicholas-wonderworker.html\">Michael the Archimandrite, <em>Life</em>, secs. 44–48</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m09\">The vial that did not break, Myra, April 1087</h3>\n<p>When men from Bari came to take the relics in April 1087, a Bari priest named Lupus dropped a glass vial of the saint’s oil on the stone floor of the church, and it did not break. The sailors took it as the saint’s consent. Status: popular tradition, told in the Greek Anonymous account and by Nicephorus of Bari.</p>\n<p>Source: <a href=\"https://sourcebooks.web.fordham.edu/basis/nicholas-bari.asp\">Greek Anonymous account of the Translation, trans. McGinley and Musurillo</a>; <a href=\"https://www.stnicholascenter.org/pages/translation-1/\">Nicephorus of Bari, <em>Translation of St Nicholas</em>, trans. Jones</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m10\">The coffin full of liquid, Myra, 1087</h3>\n<p>When Matthew of Bari broke open the tomb, the coffin was found full of liquid up to the navel of the body, and a fragrance rose so strong that men at the ships three miles away noticed it. Nicephorus of Bari, who wrote in 1087, records it. Status: recorded by an account written the same year.</p>\n<p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.stnicholascenter.org/pages/translation-1/\">Nicephorus of Bari, <em>Translation of St Nicholas</em>, trans. Jones</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m11\">Forty-seven cured on the first night, Bari, 9 May 1087</h3>\n<p>On the night the relics arrived in Bari, forty-seven people were cured, according to Nicephorus and the Greek Anonymous account. Butler gives the number as thirty, cured on the first day. Status: recorded in the accounts of the translation.</p>\n<p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.stnicholascenter.org/pages/translation-1/\">Nicephorus of Bari</a>; <a href=\"https://sourcebooks.web.fordham.edu/basis/nicholas-bari.asp\">Greek Anonymous account</a>; Alban Butler, Lives of the Saints, 6 Dec. (1866 ed.), vol. 12, pp. 91–94.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m12\">A young man possessed for five years, Bari, May 1087</h3>\n<p>Among the first cures at Bari, Nicephorus records a young man who had been possessed for five years and was deaf, mute, and blind. He was healed on the Thursday after the relics arrived. Status: recorded in an account written in 1087.</p>\n<p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.stnicholascenter.org/pages/translation-1/\">Nicephorus of Bari, <em>Translation of St Nicholas</em>, trans. Jones</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m13\">The manna of Bari</h3>\n<p>A fragrant liquid has been reported at the tomb in Bari since the translation. Butler cites a law of the emperor Manuel, reported by Balsamon, that mentions “a fragrant unctuous matter” at Bari. Each year on 9 May the clergy draw the liquid from the tomb, and a modern Orthodox compilation states that it still flows after the relics were re-examined and placed in a new tomb in 1953–1957. Status: recorded by the shrine; not independently examined in the sources read.</p>\n<p>Source: Alban Butler, Lives of the Saints, 6 Dec. (1866 ed.), vol. 12, pp. 91–94; <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/12/history-of-veneration-of-saint-nicholas.html\">“History of the Veneration of Saint Nicholas of Myra,” Mystagogy Resource Center</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"stories-of-nicholas-of-sion\">Stories later told of him that belong to Nicholas of Sion</h2>\n<p>From about the tenth century, writers merged the Life of Nicholas of Myra with that of Nicholas, abbot of Holy Sion, who died in the sixth century. The accounts below are often told of the bishop of Myra, but they come from the abbot’s Life.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m14\">The storm calmed on the pilgrimage to Jerusalem</h3>\n<p>The Vita Compilata, a ninth- or tenth-century merger of the two Lives, tells of a voyage to Jerusalem on a ship from Ascalon whose captain was named Mena. Symeon Metaphrastes added the calming of the storm on such a voyage. The episode belongs to Nicholas of Sion; Michael’s Life of the bishop has no storm on a pilgrimage. Status: popular tradition, attached to the wrong Nicholas.</p>\n<p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.stnicholascenter.org/who-is-st-nicholas/stories-legends/classic-sources/vita-compilata\">Vita Compilata, sec. 21</a>; <a href=\"https://www.stnicholascenter.org/who-is-st-nicholas/stories-legends/classic-sources/literary-development\">Pearse, “Literary Development”</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"m15\">The sailor Ammonios raised from death</h3>\n<p>Symeon Metaphrastes, in the tenth century, added the raising of a sailor named Ammonios who had died on the voyage. Like the storm, it comes from the tradition of Nicholas of Sion. Status: popular tradition, attached to the wrong Nicholas.</p>\n<p>Source: <a href=\"https://www.stnicholascenter.org/who-is-st-nicholas/stories-legends/classic-sources/literary-development\">Pearse, “Literary Development”</a>.</p>",
        "time_period": [
          "patristic_age",
          "medieval_period",
          "modern_era"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "teachings": [
      {
        "teachings": "<p>Nicholas of Myra left no writings, and no sermon or letter of his survives. What follows is the spiritual example of his life as the oldest stories remember it, not his own doctrine. The only words of his that the sources record are the short sayings that his biographers put in his mouth, and those are quoted where they belong.</p>\n<h2 id=\"giving-in-secret\">Giving in secret</h2>\n<p>In the oldest Life, Nicholas gives his inherited gold to a poor father at night, through a window, and asks only that the father tell no one. The father calls him his savior, “after God.” The example is of a gift that seeks no thanks and points beyond the giver. It is why Nicholas became the saint of gifts.</p>\n<h2 id=\"calling-oneself-a-sinner\">Calling oneself a sinner</h2>\n<p>Three times in the stories, Nicholas gives his own name with the same word: at the church door when he was chosen bishop, and in the dreams of the emperor and the prefect. Michael records the first.</p>\n<blockquote>\n  <p>“Nicholas, a sinner, a servant of your Holiness, O master.”</p>\n  <p><cite>— Nicholas at the church door, in the <em>Life</em> by Michael the Archimandrite, early ninth century</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>He said it as a young man being made bishop of the chief city of Lycia, and again, in the <em>Praxis</em>, when he appeared to the emperor on behalf of three condemned men.</p>\n<h2 id=\"defending-the-condemned\">Defending the condemned</h2>\n<p>The oldest story of Nicholas is about justice. He ran to the place of execution, took the sword from the executioner, and told the governor to his face that bribe money, not the men he blamed, had condemned the innocent. Then, when the governor repented, he forgave him. The example joins courage before the powerful with mercy toward the one who did wrong.</p>\n<h2 id=\"feeding-the-hungry\">Feeding the hungry</h2>\n<p>When famine came to Myra, Nicholas went to the grain ships at the harbor and asked each to unload a hundred measures for his city, promising that the owners would lose nothing. In Andrew of Crete’s telling he stood among the sailors as one of them and left three gold coins behind. His care took the form of practical help for a whole city.</p>\n<h2 id=\"coming-when-called\">Coming when called</h2>\n<p>Michael tells of sailors who called on Nicholas in a storm and found him among them.</p>\n<blockquote>\n  <p>“Behold, you have called me, and I am here to help you.”</p>\n  <p><cite>— Nicholas to the sailors in the storm, in the <em>Life</em> by Michael the Archimandrite</cite></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>When the same sailors found him later in his church, he told them that their greed and lust invited God’s correction. Rescue and correction went together: he helped them, and then he called them to change their lives.</p>\n<p>Sources: <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/12/the-life-of-saint-nicholas-wonderworker.html\">Michael the Archimandrite, <em>Life of Saint Nicholas</em></a>; <a href=\"https://www.johnsanidopoulos.com/2019/12/stratelatis-earliest-known-account-of.html\"><em>Praxis de stratelatis</em></a>; <a href=\"https://www.stnicholascenter.org/who-is-st-nicholas/stories-legends/classic-sources/andrew-crete\">Andrew of Crete, <em>Encomium</em></a>.</p>",
        "time_period": [
          "patristic_age"
        ]
      }
    ],
