  "gaps": [
    "Length: the biography is about 4,260 words, below the tier B range of 5,000–8,000. The notes do not support more without padding: the early life has only Michael’s Life and late legends, and Nicholas left no writings.",
    "Nicaea: attendance is not asserted. He is on Theodore the Lector’s list (c.515; authenticity disputed between Anrich and Cioffari) and on the longer lists, not on the oldest shorter lists; Michael’s Life does not mention Nicaea; Nicetas of Paphlagonia (before 890) is the first biographical mention.",
    "The slap of Arius is given one passage as late legend (first attested perhaps in the 14th century, per a tertiary source).",
    "Death year is disputed (342 Butler, 343 Golden Legend, 345 or 352 Catholic Encyclopedia and Sanidopoulos); death_year is null. Birth year is traditional (about 270); birth_year is null.",
    "Old-calendar civil equivalents of the feasts and the rank of 6 December in the Roman calendar were not sourced; both feast fields hold 6 December as the sources state it, with 2000 as the storage-only year.",
    "Bari cures: 47 (Nicephorus, Greek Anonymous) vs 30 (Butler). Raiders in 1087: Persians (Greek Anonymous) vs Turks (Nicephorus). Both stated in “A Note on the Sources”.",
    "The Praxis lines (Eustathius, Constantine, Ablabius) come from the copyrighted Jones translation; they are told in indirect speech, with no quotation marks.",
    "Bio wording to check: the governor “named” Eudoxius and Simonides as responsible is inferred from Nicholas’s reply; “he had come on behalf of the three condemned men” is inferred from the emperor freeing them; the bribe amounts are not attached to a specific half of the story.",
    "The two Michael quotations use a volunteer translation (Quinn/Pearse team) marked “short quotes only; rights: ask” in the notes. Confirm before publishing, or replace with our own translation from the Greek.",
    "relic_location uses the name “Basilica of St Nicholas, Bari”; the notes say “the new basilica” at Bari without its formal name.",
    "Single-source facts: Saracen occupation of Lycia in 1036, the manna continuing after 1953–57, and Justinian’s church (Butler dates a church to about 430). The Venetian voyage of 1100 rests on a tertiary source and a partial note in the Greek Anonymous translation.",
    "No original Greek or Latin text was read; every Greek and Latin source was read in English translation or a scholar’s summary.",
    "No image has verified rights; no prayers; only two quote records."
  ],
